Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Euclidean Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell["\<\
Euclidean Infrageometry studies natural geometric objects on arbitrary graph \
substrates using synthetic methods. It enumerates these objects, studies \
their mutual relations, applies operations to them, and performs iterative \
constructions. If space is represented by a large graph, which we may view as \
a web of paths, human geometry can be viewed as an idealized theory based on \
our large-scale perception of infinitesimality. This leads to the \
idealization of space as a \"continuous web\" supporting idealized objects \
such as the \"breadthless line.\" In contrast, Infrageometry works directly \
with the discrete substrate at an observer's scale and aggregates instead of \
subdivides. Consequently, objects at the least scale may not be unique, \
leading to multi-objects, so that constructions generate branching multiway \
systems. Although concrete representatives may differ across branches, the \
system is \"causally invariant\" in the sense that the dependency graph of \
construction steps is the same in every branch. We associate each \
multi-object with vertex and edge densities that count the representatives \
containing each vertex or edge, obtaining a measure by dividing by the total \
number of representatives. We expect that, under a suitable notion of \
Gromov-Hausdorff convergence, these measures converge weakly to Dirac \
measures supported on classical \"thin\" geometric objects. We also expect \
that a suitable axiomatization of Euclidean infrageometry can be formulated \
and the notion of  Euclidean-like graphs can be defined so that subdivision \
and convergence converges to the continuous axiomatic Euclidean geometry.\
\>", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We use inert objects such as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraSegment"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSegment", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["q", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "9f04bd34-16c8-4e69-82d6-7f19c905c17b"],
 ", independent of a graph substrate. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraMeasurement"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraMeasurement", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["obj", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["property", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2b8ec4a2-e652-4149-a062-03143fdb16f0"],
 " then computes a property of an object on a concrete graph. Each object \
also has a named sampler; for example, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["RandomInfraSegment"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraSegment", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"InfraSegment", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", " ", 
      StyleBox["q", "TI"]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["k", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "56e787c9-11fb-4aa8-b2c9-9be8fdf39169"],
 " gives up to ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " shortest paths from ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " to ",
 StyleBox["q", "TI"],
 " as vertex lists."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell["\<\
Segments, rays, lines and arcs have compact representations as directed \
acyclic graphs such that the chains correspond to individual representatives. \
This allows densities to be computed efficiently without enumerating every \
path.\
\>", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Inspired by ",
 ButtonBox["GeometricScene",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://reference.wolfram.com/language/ref/GeometricScene.html"], 
    None}],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraScene"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraScene", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "48c80b25-932d-45a6-8108-0864eb3082aa"],
 " lets us describe a construction through named objects and hypotheses. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["RandomInfraInstance"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraInstance", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["scene", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["graph", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "c030764e-b25c-4fa8-90fa-c6cf86e9cf92"],
 " draws an admissible instance on a given substrate, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraSceneViewer"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSceneViewer", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["scene", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["graph", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "a2b5d332-b2ca-4884-b6b8-ebcf95dd6550"],
 " displays the construction step by step in both the causal and branchial \
directions."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

